# Real-word typo: "lost" for "lot"/"lots" (a lost of, lost of people).
# Examples are ours.
# why: A lot of (or lots of) means many; lost means no longer found. A slip of the keys turns one into the other.
expr main [<(a lost of), lost>, <([have, has, had, got, get, with, (there are), (there were)] lost of [new, great, good, bad, other, different, small, big, interesting, exciting, fun]), lost>, <([have, has, had, got] lost of [complaints, ideas, questions, options, reasons, things, features, plans, friends]), lost>, <(lost of [people, time, money, fun, things, stuff, love, work, friends, ideas, questions, options, reasons, ways, room, space, data, information, experience, practice, effort, help, support, interest, changes, details, photos, pictures, food, water, noise, traffic, rain, snow, issues, problems, bugs, features, students, customers, users, visitors]), lost>]

let message "Did you mean `lot`/`lots`?"
let description "Catches `lost` where `lot` or `lots` was meant (a lost of people)."
let kind "Typo"
let becomes ["lot", "lots"]
let strategy "MatchCase"

test "There were a lost of people there." "There were a lot of people there."
test "We had lost of fun." "We had lot of fun."

allows "We lost a lot of time."
allows "The lost property office is closed."
allows "He is the most lost of all of us."

test "We have lost of new features planned." "We have lot of new features planned."
test "I have lost of complaints." "I have lot of complaints."
allows "We have lost a lot of time."
